================================================================================
RETIRING THE v63 DEBUG KEEPER — WHY IT IS A KILL AND NOT A UNIT EDIT
RAW EVIDENCE
zl1 (LeEco Pro3, MSM8996pro), Ubuntu Touch 24.04-2.x, 2026-09-23
OFFLINE ONLY: every line below comes from this repository's copy of the v63 boot image
(boot/v63/scripts/init-bottom/zl1-postswitch-debug-init), i.e. from the image that was flashed, plus
the port's own records. Nothing was run against the device (it was in EDL for this session) and
nothing was installed.
================================================================================


0. THE QUESTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
doc 72 section 4b identified the second heat source on this port: /usr/local/sbin/zl1-debug-net.sh,
the v63 debug network keeper — a 1 Hz /bin/sh loop that costs a full core (an A/B with it SIGSTOPped:
1.84 -> 0.87 busy cores) and makes systemd daemon-reload every ~6 s at ~2 s each. Stopping it took the
SoC down 5.5/6.1/2.7 C (tsens1/tsens8/pm8994) while the battery was still charging.

doc 72 then proposed how to retire it *durably*:

    "它的 unit 文件在 /etc/systemd/system/zl1-debug-net.service —— 是可写的，所以不用去碰只读镜像
     里的脚本，改 unit 的 ExecStart 指向一个我们自己写的、开机只跑一次的 bring-up"

That plan assumes editing that unit file survives. This file records why it does not, why the obvious
fallback (a drop-in) does not help either, and what is left.


1. THE BOOT HOOK REWRITES BOTH THE SCRIPT AND THE UNIT ON EVERY BOOT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
boot/v63/scripts/init-bottom/zl1-postswitch-debug-init, with line numbers:

      4  ROOT="${rootmnt:-/root}"                     # the REAL rootfs during initramfs

    313  cat > "$ROOT/usr/local/sbin/zl1-debug-net.sh" <<'EOF_ZL1_DEBUG_NET'
    ...
    630  EOF_ZL1_DEBUG_NET
    631  chmod 0755 "$ROOT/usr/local/sbin/zl1-debug-net.sh" 2>/dev/null || true
    632  cat > "$ROOT/etc/systemd/system/zl1-debug-net.service" <<'EOF_ZL1_DEBUG_NET_UNIT'
    633  [Unit]
    634  Description=zl1 V63 single-keeper USB debug network keeper
    635  DefaultDependencies=no
    636  After=local-fs.target systemd-udevd.service
    637  Before=basic.target usb-moded.service
    638  Conflicts=usb-moded.service
    639  [Service]
    640  Type=simple
    641  ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/zl1-debug-net.sh
    642  Restart=on-failure
    643  RestartSec=2
    644  [Install]
    645  WantedBy=sysinit.target multi-user.target
    646  EOF_ZL1_DEBUG_NET_UNIT
    647  chmod 0644 "$ROOT/etc/systemd/system/zl1-debug-net.service" 2>/dev/null || true
    648  ln -sf ../zl1-debug-net.service "$ROOT/etc/systemd/system/sysinit.target.wants/zl1-debug-net.service"
    649  ln -sf ../zl1-debug-net.service "$ROOT/etc/systemd/system/multi-user.target.wants/zl1-debug-net.service"
    650  log "installed persistent debug network keeper"

$ROOT is the real root filesystem (rootmnt while the initramfs is still running), so 313/632/648/649
write the LIVE paths. Both files — and the two enablement symlinks — are recreated unconditionally at
every boot. An edit to either is undone at the next boot.

What this does NOT touch: drop-in directories. `/etc/systemd/system/zl1-debug-net.service.d/` is never
mentioned in the hook, so a drop-in there would survive. It also outranks the /run copy (systemd's
search order is /etc/systemd/system before /run/systemd/system).


2. BUT A DROP-IN WOULD NOT HELP — THE SURVIVOR IS NOT SYSTEMD'S INSTANCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The same hook also starts the keeper DIRECTLY, before systemd exists at all:

   1587  if [ -x /usr/local/sbin/zl1-debug-net.sh ]; then
   1588      /usr/local/sbin/zl1-debug-net.sh >/dev/kmsg 2>&1 &
   1589      log "started zl1 debug network keeper from init wrapper pid=$!"

and the keeper's mutual exclusion is an atomic mkdir, not anything systemd knows about:

    329  LOCKDIR=/run/zl1-debug-net.lock
    330  LOCKPID="$LOCKDIR/pid"
    ...
    347  if mkdir "$LOCKDIR" 2>/dev/null; then
    348      record_primary_lock
    ...
    350      trap cleanup_primary EXIT HUP INT TERM
    351  else
    352      primary="$(cat "$LOCKPID" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    353      if [ -n "$primary" ] && kill -0 "$primary" 2>/dev/null; then
    354          log "keeper duplicate exit v63 pid=$$ ppid=$PPID primary=$primary ..."
    355          exit 0
    356      fi
    ...
    367  fi

and cleanup_primary removes the lock on the way out:

    340  cleanup_primary() {
    341      rc=$?
    342      trap - EXIT HUP INT TERM
    343      log "keeper exit v63 pid=$$ rc=$rc uptime=$(uptime_now)"
    344      rm -rf "$LOCKDIR" 2>/dev/null || true
    345      exit "$rc"
    346  }

So the sequence on an ordinary boot is:

    1. the ramdisk starts the keeper and it takes the lock (pid 817 in doc 72);
    2. systemd later starts its own instance from the unit; that one finds a live primary, logs
       `keeper duplicate exit v63`, and exits with status 0;
    3. `Restart=on-failure` therefore never fires, and the unit sits `inactive (dead)` for the rest
       of the boot.

This is the mechanism behind doc 72's observation that the unit "believed it exited after 67 ms" —
and it means the systemd unit is a red herring: overriding its ExecStart changes the instance that
already gives up.

INFERENCE, not a measurement: steps 2 and 3 above follow from the lock code and from doc 72's recorded
`systemctl status` (MainPID gone after 67 ms, unit not in a failed state). It was not re-measured on
the device — the device was in EDL. `install-retire-debug-keeper.sh --status` is built to show exactly
this on the next boot (it prints every keeper pid, its ppid/comm/cgroup, and the lock directory
contents), so the inference is checkable in one command.


3. WHAT IS LEFT: THE KEEPER'S OWN SHAPE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    611  while :; do
    612      write_runtime_systemd_units
    613      maybe_systemctl_mask_usb
    614      if kill_usb_managers; then
    615          force_android_usb_rndis usb-manager-killed || true
    616      else
    617          force_android_usb_rndis steady || true
    618      fi
    619      configured=0
    620      configure_iface usb0 && configured=1
    621      configure_iface rndis0 && configured=1
    622      start_debug_servers
    ...
    627      i=$((i + 1))
    628      sleep 1
    629  done

It is a plain shell script, started by the ramdisk, with no self-heal and no supervisor: its unit is
inactive, and nothing else references it. Kill the process and nothing starts it again until the next
boot, at which point the hook recreates it. That is the whole leverage, and it is honest to state what
it is: a per-boot retirement, not a durable one.

What makes the kill safe to perform:

  a. the usb-moded suppression outlives the keeper. `maybe_systemctl_mask_usb` (line 423) is
     `systemctl mask --runtime usb-moded.service` + `stop`, and the hook additionally does the blunter
     `ln -sfn /dev/null /run/systemd/system/usb-moded.service` (line 404). Both are files under /run,
     so they last for the rest of the boot. Killing the keeper does not let usb-moded back in to
     fight RNDIS.

  b. the addresses come from our own service now. zl1-netwatch.service re-asserts rndis0's addresses
     on every sample (`ensure_addrs()`, logged as `ADDRS:`), and it was installed BEFORE this on
     purpose, so the first boot that proves the new path is a boot where the keeper would have done
     the job anyway (docs 88; `scripts/device/zl1-boot-address-check.sh` is the verdict).

  c. nothing here is durable in the unsafe direction. A reboot restores the keeper exactly, because
     the hook recreates both files.

And the applier refuses to kill unless it can see an address:

    while [ "$i" -lt "$WAIT_S" ]; do has_address && break; sleep 1; i=$((i + 1)); done
    if ! has_address; then
        log "REFUSING: after ${WAIT_S}s neither 192.168.2.15/24 nor 10.15.19.82/24 is on $(iface) -- leaving the keeper running (docs 94)"
        exit 0
    fi

-- i.e. if the netwatch (and the keeper) failed to produce an address, the keeper stays. That is the
conservative direction: no address is the one state in which the keeper might still be the only thing
that can produce one.


4. THE APPLIER, AND WHAT TESTING IT OFFLINE CHANGED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`scripts/install-retire-debug-keeper.sh` installs, on the rw /etc/systemd/system path (the same path
every other unit of this port lives on):

    /etc/systemd/system/zl1-retire-debug-keeper.sh        the applier
    /etc/systemd/system/zl1-retire-debug-keeper.service   oneshot, DefaultDependencies=no,
                                                         After=local-fs.target zl1-netwatch.service,
                                                         Before=multi-user.target

Modes: `--status` (default, read-only) / `--install` (installs and enables; changes nothing on the
current boot) / `--install --now` (also retires it on the current boot) / `--remove` / `--explain`.

The applier, the kill path, the refusal path and the resurrection path were all exercised on the host
with stubs (`systemctl`, `logger`) and a fake keeper whose cmdline is `/bin/sh /tmp/fk/keeper.sh`:

    A. gate closed (no rndis0/usb0 on the host) -> REFUSED, fake keeper untouched      PASS
    B. something respawns the keeper every 4 s  -> detected and reported as a RESTART   PASS
    C. clean kill                               -> gone, verified over the watch window PASS

The first version of the split failed test B in a way worth recording, because it is the difference
between an instrument and a hazard. It matched the keeper with a substring test over the whole
cmdline:

    c=$(tr '\000' ' ' < "$d/cmdline" 2>/dev/null)
    case "$c" in *"$KEEPER"*) echo "$p" ;; esac

A substring test matches ANY process whose command line happens to mention the path — a `ps | grep
zl1-debug-net.sh`, a shell running one of this project's own scripts, an editor with the file open. In
the offline test it matched the test shell itself, and the applier killed it: the test harness died
mid-run with the tool reporting exit 144 and truncated output. On the device the same match would have
killed an unrelated process, possibly the shell someone was debugging from.

The applier now requires the path to BE an argument:

    is_keeper_cmdline() {
        set -- $(tr '\000' '\n' < "$1" 2>/dev/null)
        case "${1:-}" in
        "$KEEPER") return 0 ;;
        esac
        case "${1:-}" in
        */sh|*/dash|*/bash|*/busybox|sh|dash|bash|busybox)
            case "${2:-}" in
            "$KEEPER") return 0 ;;
            esac
            ;;
        esac
        return 1
    }

which matches the keeper's real shape (`#!/bin/sh` shebang, so its cmdline is
"/bin/sh /usr/local/sbin/zl1-debug-net.sh" — what doc 72's `ps` output showed) and nothing else. It
also skips its own pid.

The verify step was also made to distinguish two failures that have different answers, which the first
version conflated:

    survivor of the original pid set  -> "SIGKILL did not take"      (a kill problem)
    a pid that was not there before   -> "something RESTARTED the keeper as pid N -- SIGKILL was not
                                           the problem; the retirement needs a different lever (docs 94)"

The second is the one that would mean only a boot-image change retires the keeper, so it is logged as
its own fact rather than being folded into "still running".


5. WHAT WOULD RETIRE IT DURABLY (NOT DONE)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
One thing only: change the boot image. `boot/v63/scripts/init-bottom/zl1-postswitch-debug-init` is the
file that creates both the script and the unit; editing it and rebuilding the ramdisk
(`scripts/make-halium-postswitch-debug-boot.sh`) plus flashing boot would remove the keeper at the
source. That is a boot-image write, i.e. the class of change this project gates behind an explicit
decision, and it is not needed in order to get the heat down: the per-boot kill above has the same
thermal effect on every boot, at the cost of doing it again after each reboot.


6. REPRODUCTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  cd /mnt/data/zl1-bb10

  # the hook rewrites both files (lines 313, 632, 648-649) and starts the keeper itself (1587-1589)
  grep -n 'cat > "\$ROOT/usr/local/sbin/zl1-debug-net.sh"\|cat > "\$ROOT/etc/systemd/system/zl1-debug-net.service"\|zl1-debug-net.sh >/dev/kmsg' \
       boot/v63/scripts/init-bottom/zl1-postswitch-debug-init

  # the lock, the duplicate-exit path and the EXIT trap
  sed -n '329,356p' boot/v63/scripts/init-bottom/zl1-postswitch-debug-init

  # the loop and its per-second work
  sed -n '611,629p' boot/v63/scripts/init-bottom/zl1-postswitch-debug-init

  # the offline exercise of the applier (stubs + a fake keeper)
  bash scripts/install-retire-debug-keeper.sh --explain
  # and, on the device, before installing anything:
  ssh root@10.15.19.82 'sh /tmp/zl1-boot-address-check.sh'     # require: netwatch-configured (exit 0)


7. WHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * It does not verify on the device that the systemd instance is the one that exits. The lock code
    and doc 72's recorded status are consistent with it; `--status` shows it on the next boot.
  * It does not prove a keeper-less boot keeps its address. That is exactly what
    `scripts/device/zl1-boot-address-check.sh` measures, and why it is the gate.
  * It does not measure the heat difference. The applier logs the keeper's own CPU ticks
    (`/proc/<pid>/stat` fields 14+15, HZ from getconf) at the moment it retires it, so
    `scripts/device/zl1-thermal.sh --ab` has a before/after to compare — but that comparison has not
    been made.
  * It does not make the retirement durable. Nothing here changes the boot image, and nothing here
    survives a reboot except the unit that re-runs the kill.
  * Nothing was installed. The device is in EDL and this is a prepared change, awaiting a decision.
